<ProjectLayout
  slug="large-deviations"
  pageTitle="Large deviations and rare events in mean-field queueing · Akshat Valse"
  description="Rare events in mean-field JSQ(d) load balancing: large deviations choose the importance-sampling tilt, and exact computation is the benchmark where feasible."
  authors="Akshat Valse, with Ruoyu Wu"
  affiliation="Department of Mathematics, Iowa State University"
  abstract="Sending each job to the shortest of d randomly sampled queues is the standard model for randomized load balancing, and its typical behaviour has a clean mean-field description. A capacity guarantee is written against something else: the probability that some queue climbs to a high level inside a time window, which is small enough to defeat direct simulation. This project uses large-deviation theory to choose the tilt for importance sampling, and commits to checking the estimator against probabilities computed exactly, with no sampling error, at system sizes where that is still feasible. Results are held back pending submission."
  buttons={[
    { label: 'Write-up', href: WRITEUP, icon: 'github' },
    { label: 'Live figure', href: '#mechanism', icon: 'chart' },
  ]}
  info="Ongoing since November 2024 · Manuscript in preparation · Dean's High Impact Undergraduate Research Award, 2026"
>
  <Fragment slot="cover">
    <TexTile
      big
      tex={String.raw`p \;=\; \mathbb{E}_P\big[\mathbf 1_A\big] \;=\; \mathbb{E}_Q\!\Big[\mathbf 1_A\,\frac{dP}{dQ}\Big]`}
      label="The importance-sampling identity: simulate under a tilted law Q, then reweight by the likelihood ratio dP/dQ."
    />
  </Fragment>

  <!-- 1. Setting -->
  <Container first>
    <h1>The setting</h1>
    <p class="text">
      Join-the-shortest-queue-of-<Tex src="d" />, or JSQ(<Tex src="d" />), is the standard model for
      randomized load balancing. An arriving job samples <Tex src="d" /> of <Tex src="n" /> servers and
      joins the shortest of them. Sampling two servers rather than one improves the tail of the
      queue-length distribution dramatically, and as <Tex src="n" /> grows the system has a mean-field
      limit that describes typical behaviour cleanly.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      Write <Tex src="Q_i(t)" /> for the number of jobs at server <Tex src="i" /> and
      <Tex src={String.raw`s_k(t) = \tfrac1n\,\#\{i : Q_i(t) \ge k\}`} /> for the occupancy, the fraction
      of servers holding at least <Tex src="k" />. The occupancy is the empirical measure
      <Tex src={String.raw`\mu^n_t = \tfrac1n \sum_{i=1}^n \delta_{Q_i(t)}`} /> read on the sets
      <Tex src={String.raw`[k,\infty)`} />, and it is the object whose large-deviation asymptotics guide
      the project's change of measure.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      Typical behaviour is not what you provision against. What matters is the rare event: the
      probability that some queue climbs to a high level inside a time window. That is the quantity a
      capacity guarantee is written against, and the mean-field limit says nothing about it, since the
      limit describes where the system usually sits and the event of interest is a departure from
      exactly that. This page states the event in words only; its precise definition is held back with
      the manuscript.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container width="large" tone="gray">
