    <p class="caption">
      <b>Figure 1.</b> JSQ(<Tex src="d" />) dispatch, drawn for <Tex src="d = 2" /> with illustrative
      queue heights. The arriving job samples two servers (outlined) and joins the shorter (filled); the
      dashed square is where it lands. The dashed line marks a high level, and the project's quantity is
      the probability that some queue climbs to a high level inside a time window. As <Tex src={String.raw`n \to \infty`} />
      the occupancy follows the mean-field limit, and the rare event is a departure from exactly that.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container>
    <h2>Two choices against one</h2>
    <p class="text">
      How dramatic the improvement is shows up in the mean-field fixed point. Take a textbook model,
      assumed here for illustration and not taken from the project: Poisson arrivals at rate
      <Tex src={String.raw`\lambda n`} />, unit-rate exponential service, and <Tex src="d" /> servers
      sampled uniformly at random. The occupancy then follows
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\dot s_k = \lambda\,\big(s_{k-1}^d - s_k^d\big) - \big(s_k - s_{k+1}\big),\qquad s_0 = 1.`} />
    <p class="text">
      Summing the stationary equations from <Tex src="k" /> to infinity, both sides telescope to
      <Tex src={String.raw`\lambda\, s_{k-1}^d = s_k`} />, so the fixed point is explicit:
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`s_k = \lambda^{(d^k-1)/(d-1)}\quad (d \ge 2),\qquad s_k = \lambda^k \quad (d = 1).`} />
    <p class="text">
      With one sample the tail is geometric; with two it is doubly exponential. Figure 2 puts the two
      on the same axis.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container width="large" tone="gray">
    <img-comparison-slider class="slider-container no-adapt">
      <figure slot="first" class="before">
        <img class="fig no-adapt" src="/research/large-deviations/occupancy-meanfield-d1.svg" width="640" height="320"
          alt="Mean-field fixed point at load 0.9, one sample per job: the fraction of servers with at least k jobs falls geometrically, 0.9 to the k, from 1 to 0.430 at k = 8, on a log axis from 1 to 1e-12" />
        <figcaption>one sample per job (d = 1)</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure slot="second" class="after">
        <img class="fig no-adapt" src="/research/large-deviations/occupancy-meanfield-d2.svg" width="640" height="320"
          alt="Mean-field fixed point at load 0.9, two samples per job: the fraction of servers with at least k jobs falls doubly exponentially, to about 1.5e-6 at k = 7 and 2.1e-12 at k = 8, on the same log axis" />
        <figcaption>two samples per job (d = 2)</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </img-comparison-slider>
    <p class="caption">
      <b>Figure 2.</b> Classical baseline, not a project result. The mean-field fixed point of the
      textbook model at load <Tex src={String.raw`\lambda = 0.9`} />, on the
      same log axis: drag the divider. With one sample per job the fraction of servers holding at least
      <Tex src="k" /> jobs falls only geometrically, <Tex src="0.9^k" />, to 0.430 at
      <Tex src="k = 8" />. With two it falls doubly exponentially, to about
      <Tex src={String.raw`1.5\times10^{-6}`} /> at <Tex src="k = 7" /> and
      <Tex src={String.raw`2.1\times10^{-12}`} /> at <Tex src="k = 8" />. Closed form, no sampling. This
      is the typical picture, the mean-field limit; the rare event this project estimates lies outside it.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container>
    <h2>The limit against a finite system</h2>
    <p class="text">
      The fixed point is a statement about <Tex src={String.raw`n \to \infty`} />. Figure 3 sets it
      against a seeded simulation of <Tex src="n" /> servers under the same textbook model. For
      <Tex src="d = 1" /> the closed form is exact at every <Tex src="n" /> in stationarity, so the
      simulation should land on it within its error bars; for <Tex src={String.raw`d \ge 2`} /> it is only the limit, and
      the widget shows how far a small system sits from it.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container width="extra-large" tone="gray">
    <LdPowerOfChoices />
    <p class="caption">
      <b>Figure 3.</b> Classical baseline, not a project result or method. The mean-field closed form
      for a load <Tex src={String.raw`\lambda`} /> from 0.5 to 0.99 and <Tex src={String.raw`d \in \{1, 2, 3, 5\}`} />,
      against a seeded event-driven simulation of <Tex src={String.raw`n \in \{10, 100, 1000\}`} />
      servers under the textbook model above, assumed for illustration: Poisson arrivals at rate <Tex src={String.raw`\lambda n`} />, unit-rate exponential
      service, and <Tex src="d" /> servers sampled uniformly with replacement. Points are time averages,
      <Tex src={String.raw`\pm 1`} /> s.e. across independent runs; the readout gives the seed. For
      <Tex src="d = 1" /> each server receives an independent Poisson(<Tex src={String.raw`\lambda`} />)
      stream, so each queue is an M/M/1 queue with load <Tex src={String.raw`\lambda`} /> and
      <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb P(Q_i \ge k) = \lambda^k`} /> holds exactly at every <Tex src="n" /> in
      stationarity; each run starts from that law.
      For <Tex src={String.raw`d \ge 2`} /> the closed form is the <Tex src={String.raw`n \to \infty`} />
      limit, and the gap at small <Tex src="n" /> is a stationary finite-<Tex src="n" /> correction. It is
      not the time-window rare event this project estimates.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <!-- 2. Problem -->
  <Container>
    <h1>Why direct simulation fails</h1>
    <p class="text">
      These probabilities are small enough to defeat direct simulation. Crude Monte Carlo runs
      <Tex src="M" /> independent copies of the system and counts how often the event <Tex src="A" />
      occurs. Its relative error is the standard deviation over the mean:
    </p>
  </Container>

  <NoteRow>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\hat p^{\,\mathrm{crude}}_M = \frac{1}{M}\sum_{j=1}^M \mathbf 1_A(\omega_j),\qquad \mathrm{RE} = \sqrt{\frac{1-p}{M p}} \;\Longrightarrow\; M = \frac{1-p}{p\,\mathrm{RE}^2}.`} />
    <p class="text">
      An event of probability <Tex src={String.raw`10^{-20}`} /> needs something like
      <Tex src={String.raw`10^{22}`} /> independent replications before the estimator has a usable
      relative error, and it gets worse as <Tex src="n" /> grows, which is the regime you want the answer
      in. Crude Monte Carlo does not just get slow here. It stops being an estimator at all, returning
      zero occurrences and no information about the magnitude.
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="note">
      Reading "usable" as a 10% relative error, the formula gives
      <Tex src={String.raw`M \approx 10^{22}`} /> runs at <Tex src={String.raw`p = 10^{-20}`} />.
    </Fragment>
  </NoteRow>

  <Container>
    <p class="text">
      Importance sampling is the standard response: simulate under a modified law <Tex src="Q" /> that
      makes the event common, then reweight each run by the likelihood ratio, which keeps the estimate
      unbiased:
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`p = \mathbb{E}_P[\mathbf 1_A] = \mathbb{E}_Q\!\Big[\mathbf 1_A \tfrac{dP}{dQ}\Big],\qquad \hat p^{\,\mathrm{IS}}_M = \frac1M \sum_{j=1}^M \mathbf 1_A(X_j)\,\frac{dP}{dQ}(X_j),\quad X_j \sim Q.`} />
    <p class="text">
      The catch is that <Tex src="Q" /> has to be chosen well. A badly chosen change of measure can
      produce an estimator with <em>infinite</em> variance, which is worse than the naive one it replaced
      and more dangerous, because the failure does not always show up in the output. The change of
      measure needs a principle.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container>
    <h2>The mechanism, on a target with a known answer</h2>
    <p class="text">
      Figure 4 runs both estimators on the Gaussian tail <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb P(Z > a)`} />,
      <Tex src={String.raw`Z \sim \mathcal N(0,1)`} />, whose exact value <Tex src={String.raw`\Phi(-a)`} />,
      with <Tex src={String.raw`\Phi`} /> the standard normal distribution function, is known to machine
      precision. Shifting the mean to the threshold, <Tex src={String.raw`Q = \mathcal N(a,1)`} />,
      makes the event common, and the weight is explicit:
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\frac{dP}{dQ}(x) = \frac{e^{-x^2/2}}{e^{-(x-a)^2/2}} = e^{-a x + a^2/2},\qquad \hat p = \frac1M \sum_{j} \mathbf 1\{X_j > a\}\, e^{-a X_j + a^2/2},\quad X_j \sim \mathcal N(a,1).`} />
  </Container>

  <Container width="large" tone="gray" id="mechanism">
    <RareEventFigure n={4} />
    <p class="caption">
      <b>Figure 4.</b> Classical baseline: a textbook instance, chosen because its answer is exact. It
      stands in for the mechanism and is not a project result: it does not touch the project's
      estimator.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <NoteRow>
    <p class="text">
      Why the tilt works here. Write <Tex src={String.raw`W = e^{-aX + a^2/2}`} /> for the weight. Then
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}_Q\big[W^2 \mathbf 1\{X>a\}\big] = e^{a^2}\,\Phi(-2a),\qquad \frac{\operatorname{Var}_Q}{p^2} = \frac{e^{a^2}\Phi(-2a)}{\Phi(-a)^2} - 1 \;\sim\; a\sqrt{\pi/2}\quad (a\to\infty).`} />
    <p class="text">
      The relative variance per sample grows only polynomially in <Tex src="a" />. For crude Monte Carlo
      it is <Tex src={String.raw`(1-p)/p`} />, which grows like <Tex src={String.raw`e^{a^2/2}`} /> up to
      polynomial factors.
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="note">
      Key step, with <Tex src={String.raw`\varphi`} /> the standard normal density:
      <Tex src={String.raw`e^{-ax}\varphi(x) = e^{a^2/2}\varphi(x+a)`} />, so
      <Tex src={String.raw`\int_a^\infty e^{-ax+a^2/2}\varphi(x)\,dx = e^{a^2}\Phi(-2a)`} />. Textbook, and
      specific to this Gaussian stand-in.
    </Fragment>
  </NoteRow>

  <Container>
    <h2>How Figure 4 is checked</h2>
    <p class="text">
      Figure 4 is one of the site's live simulations that a verification script checks against an exact
      reference before it ships. For Figure 4:
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Target</td><td><Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb P(Z > a) = \Phi(-a),\ Z \sim \mathcal N(0,1)`} /></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Thresholds</td><td><Tex src={String.raw`a = 1, 1.5, \dots, 7`} /> (13 values)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Importance-sampling check</td><td>4,000,000 samples per threshold; <Tex src={String.raw`|z| \le 4`} /> against the exact value is required at every threshold; measured maximum 2.04</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Exact reference against SciPy</td><td>worst relative difference <Tex src={String.raw`7.2\times10^{-15}`} /> (a recorded snapshot)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Seeds</td><td><code>gaussian-tail/2026</code> for the live figure, <code>verify/raretail</code> for the check</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text">From the site's repository:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={CHECK} lang="bash" />
  </Container>

  <!-- 3. Approach -->
  <Container>
    <h1>Large deviations supply the tilt</h1>
    <p class="text">
      Large-deviation theory supplies the principle. The decay rate of the probability, and the most
      likely path along which the rare event happens, are described by a rate function built from the
      Cramér transform of the per-server net input. Knowing how the system gets to the rare state tells
      you how to tilt the dynamics so that it goes there routinely. Figure 5 shows where that sits among
      the ways to spend <Tex src="M" /> runs.
    </p>
  </Container>

