    <p class="caption">
      <b>Figure 5.</b> Three ways to spend <Tex src="M" /> runs; the dots switch panels. Crude Monte Carlo
      under <Tex src="P" /> returns zero occurrences and no information about the magnitude. Importance
      sampling tilts the law so the event becomes common and reweights each run by
      <Tex src="dP/dQ" />; large-deviation theory says which tilt, and the Gaussian case is Figure 4. A
      badly chosen tilt can give infinite variance while the output looks fine ("a few rare runs carry
      huge weights" is a sketch of the mechanism). At the other extreme, the Doob
      <Tex src="h" />-transform gives every run the same weight <Tex src="p" /> and zero variance, but
      building it requires <Tex src="h" />, which is the unknown probability itself, so in practice the
      optimal tilt has to be approximated.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <NoteRow>
    <p class="text">
      The ingredients, in textbook form. For a per-server net input <Tex src="Y" />, the log moment
      generating function and its Cramér transform are
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\Lambda(\theta) = \log \mathbb{E}\, e^{\theta Y},\qquad \Lambda^*(y) = \sup_{\theta \in \mathbb R}\big\{\theta y - \Lambda(\theta)\big\}.`} />
    <p class="text">
      For the empirical measure, the generic shape of a large-deviation principle on path space is
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\mathbb{P}\big(\mu^n \in \Gamma\big) \approx \exp\!\Big(-n \inf_{\psi \in \Gamma} I_T(\psi)\Big)`} />
    <p class="text">
      for a set <Tex src={String.raw`\Gamma`} /> of measure-valued paths, with a rate function <Tex src="I_T" /> on the horizon
      <Tex src="T" />; precisely, an upper bound for closed sets and a lower bound for open ones. The net
      input <Tex src="Y" /> for this model, its rate function and the tilt built from it are held back
      with the manuscript.
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="note">
      The textbook shape, not a result stated here for this model. It concerns events of the empirical
      measure <Tex src={String.raw`\mu^n`} />, a macroscopic object.
    </Fragment>
  </NoteRow>

  <Container>
    <h2>The optimal tilt is a Doob h-transform</h2>
    <p class="text">
      The optimal tilting is characterized by a Doob <Tex src="h" />-transform of the underlying chain.
      For a continuous-time chain with rates <Tex src="q(x,y)" />, a target set <Tex src="B" /> with
      hitting time <Tex src={String.raw`\tau_B`} />, and a horizon <Tex src="T" />, let
      <Tex src={String.raw`h(t,x) = \mathbb P(\tau_B \le T \mid X_t = x)`} />. Then
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`q^h_t(x,y) = q(x,y)\,\frac{h(t,y)}{h(t,x)},\qquad \frac{dP}{dQ^h}\Big|_{\{\tau_B \le T\}} = h(0,x_0) = p\ \text{ on every path}\;\Longrightarrow\; \operatorname{Var}_{Q^h}(\hat p) = 0 .`} />
  </Container>

  <NoteRow>
    <p class="text">
      Every path that reaches <Tex src="B" /> returns the same weight, so the estimator has zero
      variance. The catch is that <Tex src="h" /> is the unknown itself, which is why in practice it
      has to be approximated. How this project builds its tilt is held back pending submission.
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="note">
      Key step, time-homogeneous case (<Tex src={String.raw`T = \infty`} />): <Tex src="h" /> is harmonic
      on the transient states, so the total exit rate is unchanged and the likelihood ratio telescopes to
      <Tex src={String.raw`h(x_0)/h(x_\tau) = h(x_0)`} />.
    </Fragment>
  </NoteRow>

  <Container>
    <h2>What efficient means</h2>
    <p class="text">
      A standard yardstick for a rare-event estimator is logarithmic efficiency: on the exponential scale, its
      second moment decays twice as fast as the probability itself,
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\liminf_{n\to\infty} \frac{\log \mathbb{E}_Q\big[(\hat p_n)^2\big]}{\log p_n} \;\ge\; 2,`} />
    <p class="text">
      which is the best possible, since by Jensen's inequality the ratio is at most 2. Whether an estimator
      built this way is genuinely efficient, or only assumed to be, is one of the questions the project
      asks.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      Large-deviation tools reach past queueing: Sanov's theorem and the Donsker–Varadhan variational
      formula for relative entropy are the machinery behind PAC-Bayes generalization bounds. That is
      context for why the tools are worth knowing, not a claim about what this project does.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <!-- 4. Exact against asymptotic -->
  <Container>
    <h1>Exact against asymptotic</h1>
    <p class="text">
      What the project commits to methodologically is not taking any of that on faith. For system sizes
      where it is still feasible, the probability can be computed <em>exactly</em> rather than estimated:
      uniformizing the killed chain reduces the question to linear algebra on the occupancy state space,
      which gives ground truth with no sampling error. With <Tex src="B" /> made absorbing,
      <Tex src="G_B" /> the resulting generator, a rate <Tex src={String.raw`\nu \ge \max_x |G_B(x,x)|`} />
      and <Tex src={String.raw`\tilde P = I + G_B/\nu`} />,
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\mathbb{P}_x(\tau_B \le T) = \sum_{m=0}^{\infty} e^{-\nu T}\frac{(\nu T)^m}{m!}\,\big(\tilde P^{\,m}\mathbf 1_B\big)(x).`} />
    <p class="text">
      Every term lies in <Tex src="[0,1]" />, so truncating the Poisson sum leaves a Poisson tail as an
      explicit error bound. The remaining errors are truncation and rounding, never sampling. It is
      feasible only at small <Tex src="n" />.
    </p>
  </Container>

