    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container>
    <p class="text">
      Against that ground truth you can ask the questions that otherwise get waved through. Has the
      asymptotic rate actually taken hold at the system sizes anyone simulates? Is the estimator genuinely
      efficient, or only assumed to be? Do the approximations made in deriving the tilting cost anything
      that shows up in practice? Of the three, exact against asymptotic is the comparison I find most
      worth doing.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <!-- 5. What we are doing -->
  <Container>
    <h1>What we are doing</h1>
    <p class="text">
      With Ruoyu Wu in the Department of Mathematics at Iowa State, I am developing importance-sampling
      estimators for rare events in mean-field join-the-shortest-queue load-balancing systems. The
      change of measure is guided by large-deviation asymptotics for the system's empirical measure, and
      we are studying how the estimators behave as the number of servers grows.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      The estimators run on a reproducible Python simulation framework with deterministic seeding, and
      they are checked against crude Monte Carlo wherever crude Monte Carlo is reliable. Where the system
      is small enough, the project's commitment is to check them against probabilities computed exactly,
      as described above.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <!-- 6. Status -->
  <Container>
    <h1>Status</h1>
    <p class="text">
      Ongoing since November 2024, as an Undergraduate Research Fellow in the Department of Mathematics
      at Iowa State, with a Dean's High Impact Undergraduate Research Award (2026). A manuscript with
      Ruoyu Wu is in preparation:
      <em>Large-Deviation-Guided Importance Sampling for Rare Events in the Mean-Field
      Join-the-Shortest-Queue Model</em>.
    </p>
